Use `response_format` as you would with any OpenAI-compatible API:

```bash
curl http://localhost/v1/chat/completions \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OBLETH_KEY" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "model": "minimax-m2-7-fast",
    "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Extract the person: Ada Lovelace, born 1815 in London."}],
    "response_format": {
      "type": "json_schema",
      "json_schema": {
        "name": "person",
        "schema": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {
            "birth_year": {"type": "integer"},
            "city": {"type": "string"},
            "name": {"type": "string"}
          },
          "required": ["birth_year", "name"]
        }
      }
    }
  }'
```

The reply's `content` is the JSON document alone, with no prose or markdown fences around it:

```json
{"birth_year":1815,"city":"London","name":"Ada Lovelace"}
```

`"type": "json_object"` works too; the reply is then checked for valid JSON with no schema.

Send an ordinary chat request with an image to the text-only model:

```bash
curl http://localhost/v1/chat/completions \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OBLETH_KEY" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "model": "minimax-m2-7-fast",
    "messages": [{
      "role": "user",
      "content": [
        {"type": "text", "text": "What does this error dialog say?"},
        {"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgo..."}}
      ]
    }]
  }'
```

The response carries `x-obleth-boons: vision`. The request log shows two rows: a `vision_boon` call against the describer and the chat call against the model you asked for.

Callers can see the grant too. The model's entry on `GET /v1/models/{id}` keeps `supports_vision: false` and adds `boon_details.vision` with `active: true` and the describer as `helper_model`.

Before an agent runs a command, ask factual questions about the call and make the decision in code. Each question is answered on its own, without seeing the other answers. In testing, an extra `action` choice (run, ask the user, refuse) in the same request picked `run` for the `~/.cache` command below, while `in_scope` came back false at 0.9996.

```javascript
const QUESTIONS = {
  in_scope: {
    type: "boolean",
    instructions: "Does the tool call do only what the user asked for, and nothing more?",
  },
  outside_project: {
    type: "boolean",
    instructions: "Does the command delete or change anything outside the current project directory?",
  },
};

async function checkToolCall(userRequest, toolCall) {
  const r = await fetch("http://localhost:8088/v1/verdicts", {
    method: "POST",
    headers: {
      Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.OBLETH_KEY}`,
      "Content-Type": "application/json",
    },
    body: JSON.stringify({
      model: "qwen3-8b",
      state: { user_request: userRequest, tool_call: toolCall },
      questions: QUESTIONS,
    }),
  });
  if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`verdicts ${r.status}: ${await r.text()}`);
  const { verdicts } = await r.json();
  const inScope = verdicts.in_scope.probabilities.true;
  const outside = verdicts.outside_project.probabilities.true;
  return inScope >= 0.9 && outside <= 0.1 ? "run" : "ask_user";
}

