Add `images` to judge photos, screenshots or scans. Each is a `data:image/…;base64,` URL or an `http(s)` URL that the model's backend fetches itself. With images, `state` is optional.

```json
{
  "model": "auto",
  "state": "A customer's photo of the phone they are returning.",
  "images": ["data:image/jpeg;base64,/9j/4AAQSkZJRg..."],
  "questions": {
    "visible_damage": {"type": "boolean", "instructions": "Is the phone visibly damaged, such as cracked glass or a dent?"}
  }
}
```

- The model must take images itself: give it the `vision` tag, which sets `supports_vision`. Other models return 400, and `auto` picks only among models with vision. The [Vision boon](/docs/boons/vision) does not apply here.
- A request takes up to 8 images and 20 MiB of image URLs. Each question is its own call and carries the images, so image bytes times questions may be at most 64 MiB.
- The images come before the question text, so with prefix caching the backend can reuse them across the request's questions, like the state.
- Input guardrails scan the state and the questions, not the images.

[Verdict examples](/docs/clients/verdict-examples#check-a-photo) has a full request.

Redis serves what the request path needs at low latency:

- resolved keys, models (under their name and every alias) and MCP servers;
- per-minute token buckets, committed term-budget usage and in-flight term reservations, changed by atomic Lua scripts so many replicas stay correct;
- the response cache and the compression boon's stored originals, scoped per tenant;
- the live replica set and cluster-wide fairshare slots ([Multiple gateway replicas](/docs/how-it-works/fairshare-engine#multiple-gateway-replicas));
- the Slurm provisioner's heartbeat and last reconcile result, so every replica reports the same;
- the `obleth:invalidate` channel that tells every replica to drop an in-process entry.

Everything except live bucket and reservation state can be derived from Postgres, so Redis needs no backup. The gateway repopulates it at start, on every configuration write, every `OBLETH_REDIS_REWARM_SECS` (default 300) and after every reconnect, and prunes entries Postgres no longer has. `POST /api/v1/resync` does it on demand. Every key is in [Redis layout](/docs/reference/redis-layout).

Requirements:

- **A password.** The request path reads keys from Redis, so an open instance would let anyone who reaches it add keys.
- **A single primary.** The budget and slot scripts touch several keys in one call, so clustered Redis is not supported. For high availability, use a failover endpoint that presents one primary.
- **An eviction policy that spares committed state.** Under `volatil

The body is read (up to 64 MiB, else 413) and the `model` field is resolved through the same cache chain. An alias resolves to its model's canonical name, which is used from here on. `model: "auto"` goes to the [auto router](/docs/routing/auto-model-routing). Then:

- a missing model returns 400, an unregistered one 404, a disabled one 403, and one outside the tenant's allowlist 403;
- a path the gateway doesn't recognise, with no registered model, returns 404 instead of being forwarded;
- the route's [boons](/docs/boons/overview) rewrite the request (vision, knowledge retrieval, compression, tool injection, structured output, image generation, speculation);
- the tenant's input [guardrails](/docs/routing/guardrails) scan it, and a blocking match returns 400.

The token cost is estimated from the body the upstream will receive. The `x-obleth-boons: off` header skips boons for one request, never guardrails.

One atomic Redis script per scope checks and reserves the estimate:

1. the key's term budget, else 403 `api key term budget exhausted`;
2. the tenant's term budget (tokens or USD, `lifetime`, `monthly` or `term`), else 403 `tenant term budget exhausted`;
3. the tenant's per-minute token bucket (`tokens_per_minute`, 0 for no cap), else 429 `token budget exceeded`.

A term reservation holds the estimate until the request settles, so concurrent requests can't spend the same headroom; one that never settles expires after 10 minutes. On any refusal the slot is released. If Redis fails here, `OBLETH_FAIL_OPEN=true` (the default) lets the request through without enforcement and raises an alert; `false` returns 503. See [Quotas and rate limits](/docs/sharing/quotas-and-rate-limits).

On a chat model's page, find **Variants** under **General** and choose **Add a variant**.

Enter the name clients will send, a short description of what callers get, and tick the boons it adds. The line under the row says what the name reaches, for example "my-model-spec reaches my-model with Speculation turned on as well."

Save with the save bar. The boons must also be on in **Settings › Boons**, as for any model.

Or through the Management API, on `POST /api/v1/models` or `PUT /api/v1/models/{id}`:

```json
{
  "variants": [
    {
      "name": "my-model-spec",
      "description": "Faster answers, drafted by a smaller model and checked by this one",
      "boons": ["speculation"]
    }
  ]
}
```

On `PUT`, `variants` replaces the list, `[]` removes every variant, and leaving it out keeps the list.

Routing every turn afresh lets a conversation move between models as its turns change difficulty or wording, or as load shifts, and each move changes style and quality mid-task. With **Stay with a model while it answers well** on under Conversations in Settings › Routing (`sticky_enabled` on `/api/v1/settings/auto-router`, off by default), a conversation stays on the model that answered its last turn and moves up a level only when that model is not doing a good job:

- the client sends the same request again after it was answered (a retry or regenerate);
- the classifier reads the user's newest message as saying the last answer was wrong (one more single-token question, asked only on follow-up turns);
- the last answer was broken: no content and no tool call, or tool-call arguments that are not JSON;
- the model made the same tool calls three turns in a row, or two tool turns in a row came back with errors;
- the client says so with `x-obleth-auto-escalate: tool_loop` (the model it was given is going in circles) or `x-obleth-auto-escalate: complaint` (the person said the answer is wrong).

A move up raises the conversation's floor one level on its topics and leaves the model that failed out; at the top level it goes to another model there. A harder turn raises the floor to its difficulty, and the floor never drops. A quiet follow-up ("Go ahead", "thanks", a general turn) keeps the model.

